  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「フサアカシア」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%95%E3%82%B5%E3%82%A2%E3%82%AB%E3%82%B7%E3%82%A2",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109795535,
      "text": "フサアカシア とはアカシア属の常緑高木。別名、ワットルジュ、ハナアカシアともよばれる。ミモザとも呼ばれるが、本来ミモザはオジギソウを指す言葉である。中国名は銀荊。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Acacia dealbata」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia_dealbata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1367581492,
      "text": "Acacia dealbata, commonly known as silver wattle, blue wattle or mimosa, is a species of flowering plant in the legume family, Fabaceae. It is endemic to south-eastern mainland Australia but it can be found as an introduced or invasive species elsewhere. It is a bushy shrub or spreading tree with smooth bark, bluish grey or silvery and glaucous bipinnate leaves, spherical heads of yellow to bright yellow flowers, and straight to slightly curved pods. The Wiradjuri people of New South Wales use the name giigandul for the species."
    }
  },
